# Mobile/WebGL memory - notes from a shipped case (18.09.2026)

Reference for the memory budget, not a new rule. Source: "Optimizing Deep Rock Galactic: Survivor
for mobile" (https://unity.com/blog/optimizing-deep-rock-galactic-survivor-for-mobile).

- They hold ~1850 MB on a 3 GB iPad; Addressables decide what is resident, not what is loaded.
- Damage numbers as a ParticleSystem + a digit sprite atlas instead of TMP text objects.
- Enemy animation baked into a texture and animated in the vertex shader, plus GPU instancing.
- Flow field instead of NavMesh; KD-tree instead of heavy physics queries at scale.
- Custom batched draw for procedural geometry - the same class of problem as our procedural shapes.
- Automated capture scenarios + Profiler runs, not eyeballing.

Use these when we measure the WebGL/mobile memory budget after the burn campaign, together with
UUM-149540 (`Resources.UnloadUnusedAssets` in players) and the Light2D scene-unload leak (the release
notes put that fix in 6000.6.2, the issue tracker lists only 6000.7.0b1 - sources disagree, verify at the
upgrade).

# WebGL pre-build: output-asset size limit (28.09.2026)

A WebGL build fails hard when the summed output assets pass 2 GB ("System.IO.IOException: The file is too
long." - "This operation is currently limited to supporting files less than 2 gigabytes in size"), and
outputs above 4 GB are unsupported in any version (UUM-112909 / issue 6820). The 2 GB fix sits in
`6000.6.3f1` and `6000.7.0a4` - we are on 6.6.3 since 30.09, so the limit is lifted; keep the pre-build
sum anyway and keep headroom under 2 GB.

One trap for the same metric (02.10): content directories are **not compressed by default** while
AssetBundles are, so a naive switch shows the new pipeline as "bigger". Set compression explicitly
(Addressables 4.1: Content Directory Archive Mode = LZ4 / Uncompressed / None) and compare all three
modes against the current AssetBundle build for size and time together.

# WebGL pre-build: Web Memory Size (29.09.2026)

Keep the Web Memory Size setting under 4 GB: above it the player switches to Wasm64 - no threads and a
noticeable performance penalty (Unity staff in the WebGPU/Wasm64 forum thread, fetched via the 29.09
digest). Check it before the first WebGL build, together with the output-asset sizes above.

# Android pre-build: 16 KB page sizes (21.09.2026)

Google Play rejects an AAB when any third-party `.so` is not 16 KB aligned. The engine side is fine:
Unity supports 16 KB page sizes from 6000.0.38f1, so our 6000.5.8f1 is in.

Our state, verified 21.09.2026: `com.unity.burst` resolves to **1.8.30** (`Library/PackageCache`,
`packages-lock.json`) where 1.8.21+ is required, and `Assets` contains no `.so`, `.aar` or `.jar` at
all - the only plugin folder is `Assets/Plugins/WebGL`. Nothing can misalign today.

Keep it as a one-time check before the first Android build and repeat it whenever a third-party
plugin or SDK lands: open the AAB with APK Analyzer, look at `lib/arm64-v8a`, confirm the alignment.
Retired SDKs are the usual offenders in that thread (e.g. NiceVibrations).

# Build-loop candidate: Unity CLI recompile (23.09.2026)

Installed CLI is `1.0.0-beta.9` (`~/AppData/Local/Unity/bin/unity`): it exposes `build`, `run`, `test`,
`job`, `command`, `pipeline`, `status`, `doctor` - but NOT `recompile`. beta.11 (2026-09-22) adds
`unity recompile`: a compile check without a full build that lists errors with file and line, exit
codes 0/6/7, plus `--strict` (fails on warnings too). That is a cheaper pre-gate than the current
10-minute full batch build.

Do NOT swap the compile gate while the burn campaign is live - the gate is bound to the harness.
After the campaign: reinstall the CLI (the thread reports `unity self-update` does not cross that
range cleanly) and A/B `unity recompile` against the existing gate before wiring it in.

# Mobile pre-flight checklist (before the first Android/iOS build, 22.09.2026)

Verified in the repo on 22.09.2026, so these are re-checks with a known answer, not open risks:

- Render pipeline / quality: `ProjectSettings/QualitySettings.asset` has 6 levels (Very Low, Low,
  Medium, High, Very High, Ultra); only Ultra assigns a pipeline asset (`Assets/Settings/URP-2D.asset`),
  the other five fall back to `GraphicsSettings.m_CustomRenderPipeline`, which is the same URP-2D asset.
  One asset for every platform - nothing diverges desktop vs Android today. Re-check after adding a
  mobile-specific URP asset: the classic symptom (22.09 thread) is that switching the platform to
  Android changes the picture through a mobile URP asset in Quality Settings.
- Managed stripping: `ProjectSettings/ProjectSettings.asset` has `managedStrippingLevel: {}` (Unity
  per-platform defaults, not Medium), and the project holds 0 `[Preserve]` attributes, 0
  `GetCustomAttribute` calls and no DI framework (Zenject/UniDi/VContainer absent). So the reported
  6000.5+ bug - Medium stripping drops `CustomAttribute` entries on attributes derived from
  `UnityEngine.Scripting.PreserveAttribute`, visible only on device, silent in the editor - cannot
  reach us. Re-check if a DI framework or Preserve-derived attributes enter, or if stripping is
  raised to Medium.
- Native dependencies: `Assets` holds no `.so`/`.aar`/`.jar` (see the 16 KB section above). When the
  first native dependency arrives, `com.unity.external-dependency-manager` 2.1.0 (official Unity
  package, UPM, 2022.3+; announced 2026-09-29) is the manager to use, and the same place where we would
  control what goes into `lib/arm64-v8a`. Deadline pressure for other projects: Google deprecates EDM4U
  on 2026-10-26 and EDM replaces it with the same XML declaration format - we hold no EDM4U setup, so
  nothing to migrate today; the note exists so the first native SDK lands on EDM, not on EDM4U.
- Android minimum spec from 6.6 (2026-09-30): the minimum is OpenGLES 3.1, and the "Require ES 3.1"
  Player Setting is removed - all Android players go through Vulkan or GLES 3.1/3.2, with Vulkan
  recommended and automatic fallback. Unity's own thread quotes the reach cost: GLES 3.0 cuts ~0.4% of
  players, 3.1 ~0.7%. We have no Android build yet, so this is a note for the first mobile config.
- 2D light budget (2026-09-30): Unity staff say web platforms get the mobile shader limits and that the
  per-camera limit on additional lights can be raised via the URP Config package, while Forward+ removes
  the 8-light-per-object cap (Deferred is not available on WebGL). Our case is the 2D Renderer, where the
  binding number is our own `Assets/Settings/Renderer2D.asset`: `m_MaxLightRenderTextureCount: 4` with
  `m_LightRenderTextureScale: 0.5`. Scenes hold 0 Light2D components; lights are created at runtime
  (Campfire, Torch, SceneLighting), at most one per scene (TreeApple, Clearing, Campfire) - so we sit far
  under the budget. The URP asset also carries `m_AdditionalLightsRenderingMode: 0` (disabled), i.e. the
  3D additional-light cap does not apply to us at all. Re-check the count if a scene ever lights several
  sources at once.
- Android system bars / immersive (checked 2026-09-27): since Unity 6.5 `Screen.fullScreen` "no longer
  has an effect on Android" - Unity's own 6.5 upgrade guide says bar visibility must be driven through
  `AndroidApplication.currentWindowInsets.Show/Hide`, and `PlayerSettings.Android.renderOutsideSafeArea`
  now only covers the cutout. On top of that there is a real regression: once a tap reveals the bars
  they can stick and immersive mode breaks (thread 1735775, active 25.09); Unity staff say the fix
  shipped in 6.7.0b1 and is coming to 6.6. Our exposure today is zero - `Assets` has 0
  `Screen.fullScreen`, 0 `AndroidApplication`/`currentWindowInsets` and no `UNITY_ANDROID` blocks - so
  this is a rule for the first mobile build, not a fix: never drive the bars through the old fullscreen
  API, use `currentWindowInsets`.
